---
/**
 * /cvp-vs-vcp — Disambiguation page for the two acronyms.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageContainer from '../components/PageContainer.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import CrossCitation from '../components/CrossCitation.astro';
import Implementers from '../components/Implementers.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="CVP vs VCP"
  description="Customer Value Platform (CVP) versus Value Creation Protocol (VCP). Disambiguation: one is the platform category, the other is the protocol that runs on top of it."
  path="/cvp-vs-vcp"
  ogImage="og-cvp-vs-vcp.jpg"
  schemaType="DefinedTerm"
  schemaTermCode="CVP vs VCP"
  schemaDefinedTerms={[
    {
      name: 'Customer Value Platform',
      termCode: 'CVP',
      description:
        'A platform category — software organized around customer relationships and value creation rather than internal record-keeping. HubSpot is the canonical implementing CVP for the Value-First method.',
    },
    {
      name: 'Value Creation Protocol',
      termCode: 'VCP',
      description:
        'The methodology layer of the AI-native protocol stack. Sits atop MCP (capability) and HCP (human context). Gives organizations the operating discipline to translate AI capability and human context into realized value.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <PageContainer>
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="Disambiguation"
      title="CVP vs VCP"
      lede="Two acronyms, one letter off, completely different layers. Here is the boundary."
    />

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-12">
      <article class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 bg-white p-6 md:p-7">
        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.15em] font-semibold text-vcp-teal m-0 mb-3">
          Customer Value Platform
        </p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 m-0 mb-3">CVP</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          A <strong>platform category</strong>. Software organized around customer
          relationships and value creation rather than internal record-keeping.
          HubSpot is the canonical implementing CVP for the Value-First method.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-600 mt-4 mb-0 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          When you hear "CVP," think substrate. The software your customer-facing
          team operates from. Configuration-driven, native-object-first.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-6 mb-0">
          <a href="/hubspot-cvp" class="text-stone-700 underline text-sm">HubSpot as CVP →</a>
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 bg-white p-6 md:p-7">
        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.15em] font-semibold text-vcp-orange m-0 mb-3">
          Value Creation Protocol
        </p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 m-0 mb-3">VCP</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          A <strong>protocol</strong>. The methodology layer of the AI-native
          protocol stack. Sits atop MCP (capability) and HCP (human context).
          Gives organizations the operating discipline to translate AI capability
          and human context into realized value.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-600 mt-4 mb-0 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          When you hear "VCP," think methodology. The operating protocol the
          system runs. Open, platform-agnostic, declarable.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-6 mb-0">
          <a href="/positioning" class="text-stone-700 underline text-sm">Positioning paper →</a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <article class="prose mx-auto">
      <h2>The boundary</h2>
      <p>
        CVP is what you operate <em>on</em>. VCP is what you operate <em>by</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A team using HubSpot (a CVP) without VCP has the platform but no
        operating discipline — they will configure the platform around
        industrial-age defaults because that's what the foundation models in
        their AI tooling want to do, and that's what the historical training
        data of the platform suggests.
      </p>
      <p>
        A team committed to VCP without a CVP has the operating discipline
        but no machine-executable substrate. They can teach the method to
        humans, but AI systems they deploy will default to the vocabulary
        VCP rejects.
      </p>
      <p>
        VCP plus a configured CVP is the operating arrangement. The CVP is
        the substrate where VCP runs. Other substrates are possible — the
        protocol is platform-agnostic — but HubSpot is the canonical
        implementing CVP for the Value-First method because it has the
        native objects, the configuration depth, and the public
        accessibility to make the configuration-over-customization principle
        operable.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the naming hurts</h2>
      <p>
        The acronyms differ by a single letter and live in adjacent
        conceptual space. This is unfortunate and intentional: both names
        come from the same lineage. CVP names the platform category that
        VCP runs on top of. They were never meant to be heard in isolation,
        and they aren't competing for the same slot.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you need to be exact, say it long: "Customer Value Platform"
        for the substrate, "Value Creation Protocol" for the methodology.
        When you're working inside the team, context disambiguates.
      </p>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/hubspot-cvp">HubSpot as CVP — the canonical implementing substrate</a></li>
        <li><a href="/positioning">VCP Positioning Paper — where VCP sits in the protocol stack</a></li>
        <li><a href="/encoding-stack">Encoding Stack — what makes VCP machine-parseable</a></li>
        <li><a href="/glossary">Glossary — full term index</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <CrossCitation path="/cvp-vs-vcp" />
    <Implementers />
  </PageContainer>
</BaseLayout>
